{
  "image_name": "1d3fda486a2e9caf6ca4918c6dcb8c2b.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the basic design of a laser interferometric gravitational-wave detector and the way its main mirrors are suspended. On the left, a stabilized laser is sent through input optics and a beam splitter into two perpendicular 4 km arms, forming Fabry–Pérot arm cavities with input and end test masses. Power- and signal-recycling cavities, plus input and output mode cleaners and a photodiode, are shown as part of the optical layout. A small schematic above illustrates the effect of a passing gravitational wave: space is stretched in one direction and compressed in the other, so the relative arm lengths change and the interference signal at the output varies.\n\nOn the right, the test masses at the ends of the interferometer are depicted as multi-stage pendulum suspensions about 1.6 m tall. Upper stages use metal masses and steel wires, while the lower stages use fused silica masses hung by fused silica fibers to isolate the optics from ground vibration. The laser beam enters through the input test mass (ITM), travels along the 4 km arm, and reflects from the end test mass (ETM). An electrostatic actuator near the input optic appears to provide fine control of mirror position.",
